# The `nobilus` build profile is the Ubuntu 22.04 artefact (ruling 2026-11-09,
# issue #289): it drops every fcitx5 build dependency and the fcitx5 binary
# package, leaving libibus-1.0-dev + the core's own dependencies. It is used by
# the `ibus-jammy` CI job on a real ubuntu-22.04 runner with
# `dpkg-buildpackage -Pnobilus`, and dpkg-checkbuilddeps is asserted there
# without -d. The default build keeps the noble package set unchanged.
Standards-Version: 4.6.2

Package: libhunmin
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Description: portable Hangul input engine (shared library)
 libhunmin is the portable Hangul input engine whose keyboard layouts are
 data files rather than code. It composes syllables and buffers an eojeol,
 evaluates the Hangul/English judgement, and serves Hanja candidates.
 .
 This package carries the shared library the frontends link against. It is
 a separate runtime package so the fcitx5 addon and the IBus engine can be
 installed independently -- the IBus-only Ubuntu 22.04 release must not
 pull in fcitx5 merely to obtain the core.

Package: hunmin-data
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends}
Description: keyboard layouts and dictionaries for the hunmin input engine
 The layout tables, composition rules, Hanja dictionaries and idiom tables
 that libhunmin reads at runtime. Kept frontend-neutral so that an IBus-only
 installation obtains its data without bringing in a fcitx5 dependency.
 .
 The English word list is a separate package (hunmin-en-us), as before.

Package: hunmin
Build-Profiles: <!nobilus>
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}, libhunmin (= ${binary:Version}), hunmin-data (= ${binary:Version}), fcitx5 (>= 5.1.7)
Recommends: hunmin-en-us
Description: Korean input method engine (libhunmin core + fcitx5 addon)
 libhunmin is a portable Hangul input engine where keyboard layouts are
 data files rather than code. It matches libhangul behaviour for the
 standard layouts (golden-vector and parity tests) and implements
 신세벌식 P2 (sin3-p2) including symbol tiers, 초성체 and 옛한글 rules.
 .
 This package ships the shared library, the hunmin-sim trace simulator,
 the layout/compose data files, and the fcitx5 addon (addon and input
 method name "hunmin", coexisting with fcitx5-hangul).
 .
 The English word list used by the automatic Hangul/English switch ships
 as hunmin-en-us, and this package Recommends it, so a plain install has one
 dictionary and the feature can be used before a distribution mechanism for
 additional dictionaries exists (ruling 2026-11-01; SPEC-MODES R8-정정-2, B3).
 A machine without it is still a normal installation: the engine answers "no
 English words" and every mode keeps working.

Package: hunmin-en-us
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends}
Multi-Arch: foreign
Description: hunspell en_US word list for hunmin's Hangul/English switch
 The English word set behind hunmin's automatic Hangul/English switch
 (SPEC-MODES R8-정정's english_valid). It is hunspell en_US expanded one
 affix level, lowercased and sorted, generated by tools/en-us2data.py and
 shipped as /usr/share/hunmin/modes/ next to its LICENCE and provenance.
 .
 It is a separate package on purpose: the ruling that created the feature
 makes its data a separate distribution, so that the switch can be enabled
 only where the data is present (B3). With this package absent, the engine
 answers "no English words" and every mode keeps working; see
 /usr/share/hunmin/modes/meta.json for the source, the method and what the
 list does not represent.
 .
 Licence: SCOWL's permission notice plus the hunspell .aff's BSD, both
 reproduced in full in /usr/share/hunmin/modes/LICENSE.md and in
 /usr/share/doc/hunmin-en-us/copyright.

Package: hunmin-ibus
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}, libhunmin (= ${binary:Version}), hunmin-data (= ${binary:Version}), ibus (>= 1.5.26)
Recommends: hunmin-ibus-settings
Description: Korean input method engine for IBus (Ubuntu 22.04 release frontend)
 hunmin is a portable Hangul input engine where keyboard layouts are data
 files rather than code. This package ships its IBus engine: a real IBus
 component that composes through the shared hunmin layer and publishes
 preedit, commits and the Hanja lookup table on IBus's own interfaces.
 .
 This is the frontend of the Ubuntu 22.04 release, which ships no fcitx5
 dependency at all (decision 2026-11-09, issue #289). The existing `hunmin`
 package keeps shipping the fcitx5 addon unchanged; the two are separate
 binary packages so `apt install hunmin` means the same thing on every
 target. The core library is the separate `libhunmin` runtime package.
 .
 The layout/compose data and its licence and provenance statements ship in
 `hunmin-data`; this engine reads them from /usr/share/hunmin.
 .
 This package also carries the GSettings schema the engine's five option rows
 live in. The window that edits them is a separate package (hunmin-ibus-settings)
 because it needs a toolkit the engine does not.

Package: hunmin-ibus-settings
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}, hunmin-ibus (= ${binary:Version})
Recommends: hunmin-en-us
Description: settings window for the hunmin IBus engine
 The five option rows the IBus engine is configured by — the Hangul and Latin
 layouts, the commit unit, the automatic Hangul/English switch, and the keys
 that switch the language — as a window the IBus panel opens from its
 Preferences item.
 .
 It is a separate package because it needs a toolkit: an installation that only
 runs the engine pulls none of that in, which is what the Ubuntu 22.04 artefact
 requires (decision 2026-11-09, issue #289). The rows are stored in a GSettings
 schema, the same one the engine reads, so a change takes effect without
 restarting anything.
